"1. Load MovieLens100K ratings from the local './u.data' file using the correct tab-separated format and assign columns for user, item, rating, and timestamp.\n2. Use LensKit\u2019s ImplicitMF recommender for all runs, keeping every hyperparameter fixed except the random seed.\n3. Create a single 80/20 train/test split protocol that is reproducible and explicitly controlled, so seed effects are isolated to model randomness rather than changing data splits.\n4. Ensure the training data is converted to the implicit-feedback format expected by ImplicitMF, with a clear and consistent rule for deriving interactions from ratings.\n5. Train the model repeatedly over a sufficiently large set of different random seeds (e.g., at least 10 runs) using identical training and evaluation settings.\n6. Generate top-N recommendations for the same test users in every run, excluding items already seen in training for each user.\n7. Evaluate each run with ranking metrics appropriate for top-N recommendation, including at minimum NDCG and Recall at a fixed cutoff K.\n8. Use a consistent relevance definition for evaluation on the test set and document it in code (e.g., all held-out interactions or only ratings above a threshold).\n9. Record per-seed metric results and aggregate them into a summary table with mean, standard deviation, minimum, maximum, and/or confidence intervals across runs.\n10. Quantify seed sensitivity by reporting variability across runs and comparing its magnitude to the mean performance, so the experiment can assess whether seed reporting matters.\n11. Include enough logging or printed output to show the seed used for each run and the corresponding metric values.\n12. Handle users with insufficient test interactions or evaluation edge cases in a way that keeps comparisons across seeds valid and consistent.\n13. Ensure the implementation is executable end-to-end in the current working directory without manual file edits, assuming 'u.data' is present and required Python packages are installed.\n14. Structure the code so that experimental settings (number of seeds, cutoff K, relevance threshold, model hyperparameters, split seed) are easy to modify and clearly defined in one place.\n15. Produce a concise final interpretation from the computed variability statistics indicating whether random seeds materially affect the reported recommender results in this setup."